From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:32:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 137/148] fix(polecat): take over a dead owner's work bead on
 resume and check the refinery handoff (gcp-fooz4)

bd >= 1.3.0 (live on the host) refuses a plain --assignee write over
another actor's in_progress claim unless it carries --if-assignee <current>
or --force (bd update --help, 1.3.0). The WORK bead is held in_progress
for the whole run (0060), so whenever it sits under a spelling other than
this session's actor, submit-and-exit's step-6 handoff to the refinery is
refused. Two ways in: the live-owner guard's resume arm (0085) lets a new
session proceed past a dead owner without re-claiming the bead, and the
gc 1.4.3 -> 1.5.0 identity spelling change. The step-6 write was
unchecked, so the refusal left the branch pushed, the bead never reached
the refinery, and steps 7-8 drained the session.

- The resume arm now takes the WORK bead over with a compare-and-set on the
  owner it just found dead: --assignee=$EXPECTED_ASSIGNEE plus
  polecat_session, --if-assignee "$GUARD_OWNER". Like workspace-setup's
  claim it never takes a bead from the refinery target or one routed to
  human. A refused takeover warns and the resume proceeds.
- Step 6 checks the handoff write. On refusal it prints HANDOFF_FAILED
  naming the bead, tells the agent not to run steps 7-8 or drain-ack, and
  exits 1 before the status read-back.

Tests, red before and green after:
- test_polecat_live_owner_guard.sh
  test_dead_owner_resume_takes_the_work_bead_over: red, no takeover call
  in the stub log; test_refused_takeover_warns_and_still_resumes: red, no
  WARN. test_resume_never_takes_over_from_the_refinery_or_an_escalation
  pins the two skips.
- test_polecat_handoff_stamp.sh test_refused_handoff_fails_loudly: red,
  "a refused handoff must print a HANDOFF_FAILED line naming the bead".
Two pack-asset ordering checks now anchor the polecat_session stamp to
"$WORK_ID", since the takeover in the guard also names this session.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
